{
  "requirement": "The `habeas-requirements` skill MUST tell agents that drafts may be high-level, compound, or incomplete, and that they need not answer every question before review.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The requirement has two obligations: the `habeas-requirements` skill must tell agents that drafts may be high-level, compound, or incomplete, and that they need not answer every question before review.\n\n`tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_requirements_skill_allows_rough_drafts_before_review]` carries the `{test}` reference. It finds the skill's \"Write drafts\" workflow step with `step_of`, then extracts that step's text with `step`. It then uses `assert_text` to require two phrases in that step:\n- \"Drafts may be high-level, compound, or incomplete\". This covers the first obligation, including all three qualities.\n- \"Ask the user only what the request can't answer; the review in step 3 finds the rest\". This covers the second obligation. It says the agent need not have every question answered before review, because the review finds the rest.\n\nThe helpers `step`, `step_of` and `assert_text` assert real content. `step` and `step_of` panic if the step is missing. `assert_text` fails if a phrase is absent. The test would therefore fail if the skill dropped either message or moved it out of the draft step.\n\nThe requirement has no other modes, boundaries or failure cases. A phrase-presence check is the natural way to test an obligation about what the skill text says. I found no gap.",
  "references": "6193a1898ebe807ae000c7fc0e0dc0474178b0cccf783521f25e427c6e1293f8",
